hether changing the window size would be a workaround for a real product defect, since the feature only checks messaging behavior and not layout. The fix more likely belongs in the test's synchronization, so I'm considering waiting for the drawer to be fully open before interacting with it.\n\nI'm considering whether the search \"field\" click is actually hitting a toggle button in the drawer header that opens a separate search panel, which would explain why the backdrop from that panel intercepts the later click on the input. The backdrop might come from the messaging toggle opened at the start rather than from the drawer itself, so I'm weighing which element owns it.\n\nI'm wondering whether the homepage carries its own modal, like a welcome or cookie notice, though that seems unlikely for this site. I could try scrolling or focusing the search input first, but that wouldn't change anything meaningful. Another option is to check whether the search runs on keyup or enter in the new UI, which would make the click on the search control unnecessary, though dropping it would change what the step actually does. I'm also thinking about how the upstream Moodle step handled this same search case.\n\nI'm now looking at whether a fixed wait or a check on the search input's actual clickability would be more reliable than changing the window size.\n\nI'm weighing a single wait right after opening messaging, checking that the drawer is visible and its backdrop gone, instead of waiting before the search step. That wouldn't fit, though, because the backdrop stayed in place for ten seconds after the search field was filled.\n\nThe backdrop looks persistent with the drawer open, so the search input sits under it, probably because the drawer's stacking order is lower. That reads like a product issue rather than something the test can fix. Resizing the window only makes sense if the drawer behaves as a modal at small widths, which doesn't match what I'm seeing here.\n\nI'm wondering 